1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,376
Every person and every service on a Linux server runs as a user.

2
00:00:04,396 --> 00:00:07,935
Create the wrong account, or forget to delete an old one,

3
00:00:07,955 --> 00:00:09,745
and you have a security hole.

4
00:00:10,215 --> 00:00:14,334
In this video: create, delete, and modify local user accounts.

5
00:00:18,854 --> 00:00:21,437
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:21,907 --> 00:00:25,087
Create, delete, and modify local user accounts.

7
00:00:25,557 --> 00:00:29,602
We'll create a user, create one with options, change an existing user,

8
00:00:29,622 --> 00:00:32,854
and delete users, with and without their home directory.

9
00:00:40,374 --> 00:00:43,867
Every local user is one line in the file etc passwd.

10
00:00:43,887 --> 00:00:48,934
You see a line with getent passwd. It has seven parts, separated by colons:

11
00:00:48,954 --> 00:00:53,865
the name, a password placeholder, the user I D, the group I D, a comment,

12
00:00:53,885 --> 00:00:56,567
the home directory, and the login shell.

13
00:01:06,037 --> 00:01:11,759
Three commands do the job. User add creates. User mod modifies. User del deletes.

14
00:01:11,779 --> 00:01:16,296
We use sudo, because only the administrator can change accounts.

15
00:01:16,316 --> 00:01:19,628
In this demo, the lab changes nothing silently.

16
00:01:27,148 --> 00:01:31,512
Create a user: sudo user add alice. No output means it worked.

17
00:01:31,532 --> 00:01:36,533
The command id shows her user I D and group I D, both one thousand one.

18
00:01:36,553 --> 00:01:42,402
Getent passwd alice shows her line, with the home directory slash home slash alice.

19
00:01:42,422 --> 00:01:47,352
And ls dash d lists that directory itself: user add created it for us.

20
00:02:00,872 --> 00:02:05,078
Options let you decide the details. Dash u sets the user I D.

21
00:02:05,098 --> 00:02:10,344
Dash c sets the comment, usually the full name. Dash s sets the login shell.

22
00:02:10,364 --> 00:02:15,956
The line shows user I D fifteen hundred, the comment Bob Lee, and the shell bash.

23
00:02:26,476 --> 00:02:31,014
To change an existing user, use user mod, with the same options.

24
00:02:31,034 --> 00:02:36,853
Dash s with slash user slash sbin slash no login means this user can no longer log

25
00:02:36,873 --> 00:02:41,411
in. Dash c changes the comment. The line now shows both changes.

26
00:02:50,931 --> 00:02:56,295
To delete a user: user del bob. Getent now finds nothing: the account is gone.

27
00:02:56,315 --> 00:03:01,748
But ls shows the home directory is still there. User del alone keeps the files.

28
00:03:10,268 --> 00:03:13,452
To remove the home directory too, add dash r.

29
00:03:13,472 --> 00:03:18,222
We create carol, then delete her with user del dash r. Now ls says:

30
00:03:18,242 --> 00:03:21,640
no such file or directory. The home is gone too.

31
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:34,596
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

32
00:03:36,116 --> 00:03:40,523
We reboot. Alice is still there, with her changes. Bob is still gone.

33
00:03:40,543 --> 00:03:44,501
Account changes are saved in a file, so they survive a reboot.

34
00:03:52,021 --> 00:03:57,785
Your turn. Create the user sam, with user I D three thousand one and the comment Sam Smith.

35
00:03:57,805 --> 00:04:00,074
Change the shell of sam to no login.

36
00:04:00,094 --> 00:04:03,315
Delete the user temp, including the home directory.

37
00:04:03,335 --> 00:04:07,701
The lab silently creates temp first, so you have something to delete.

38
00:04:07,721 --> 00:04:10,625
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

39
00:04:21,095 --> 00:04:22,846
Try before you open the solution.

40
00:04:28,366 --> 00:04:33,146
Next up: change passwords and adjust password aging for local user accounts.

41
00:04:33,616 --> 00:04:36,519
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
